From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Boris Krasnovskiy <boris.krasnovskiy@ezurio.com>
Date: Sun, 2 Aug 2026 00:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] fs_crypt: accept trusted keys via key_id

FS_IOC_ADD_ENCRYPTION_KEY already supports specifying a master key by
Linux keyring ID (key_id) instead of raw bytes, but only accepts keys
of type "fscrypt-provisioning", whose payload must already contain
raw key bytes supplied by userspace.

Widen this to also accept Linux "trusted" keys (TPM- or TEE-sealed).
A trusted key never exposes its raw payload to userspace via keyctl
(read/pipe/print all return only the sealed export blob), so this
allows a persistently-sealed trusted key to be used as an fscrypt v2
master key while the raw key material never leaves kernel space.

Reject hardware-bound trusted keys (is_hw_bound) since their payload
is not a directly-usable raw key, and bound the accepted key size to
fscrypt's actual raw-key limits (FSCRYPT_MIN_KEY_SIZE..
FSCRYPT_MAX_RAW_KEY_SIZE) rather than the secret buffer's storage
size, which is sized for the larger of raw or hardware-wrapped keys.

PROD-62700
---
 fs/crypto/keyring.c | 28 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 28 insertions(+)

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate Local requirement

--- a/fs/crypto/keyring.c	2026-08-02 02:31:17.183248839 -0400
+++ b/fs/crypto/keyring.c	2026-08-02 02:31:27.972657667 -0400
@@ -25,6 +25,7 @@
 #include <linux/random.h>
 #include <linux/seq_file.h>
 #include <linux/unaligned.h>
+#include <keys/trusted-type.h>
 
 #include "fscrypt_private.h"
 
@@ -701,6 +702,29 @@
 		return PTR_ERR(ref);
 	key = key_ref_to_ptr(ref);
 
+	/*
+	 * Allow a Linux "trusted" key (e.g. TPM- or TEE-sealed) to be used
+	 * directly as a v2 fscrypt master key, in addition to the normal
+	 * "fscrypt-provisioning" key type.  Trusted keys never expose their
+	 * raw payload to userspace, so the raw key never leaves the kernel.
+	 * Only allow this for non-hardware-wrapped v2 keys, since trusted
+	 * keys are not hardware-wrapped keys in the fscrypt sense.
+	 */
+	if (key->type == &key_type_trusted) {
+		const struct trusted_key_payload *tpayload;
+
+		if (type != FSCRYPT_KEY_SPEC_TYPE_IDENTIFIER || flags != 0)
+			goto bad_key;
+		tpayload = key->payload.data[0];
+		if (tpayload->key_len < FSCRYPT_MIN_KEY_SIZE ||
+		    tpayload->key_len > FSCRYPT_MAX_RAW_KEY_SIZE)
+			goto bad_key;
+		secret->size = tpayload->key_len;
+		memcpy(secret->bytes, tpayload->key, secret->size);
+		err = 0;
+		goto out_put;
+	}
+
 	if (key->type != &key_type_fscrypt_provisioning)
 		goto bad_key;
 	payload = key->payload.data[0];
